# Architecture tests

### Rules about the code, kept as code

Tutors Ladder, rung 3 (Layers), talk 3

---

## A diagram is L2

The README of rung 3 draws the layers and lists who may import what. A coding agent may read it, forget it,
or decide that "just this once" `lite` can import a helper from `gen`.

```
apps/cli ──► @ladder/gen ──► @ladder/model
    │                            ▲
    └──────► @ladder/lite ───────┘
```

`AGENTS.md` says it plainly: "The layering, the port rule and the README's Architecture section say the
same thing; the tests decide."

So each rule in the diagram climbs to L3, a test, with an L4 message.

---

## The table the rules read

```ts
// variants/rung-3-layers/tests/architecture/architecture.test.ts
/** Each layer, where it lives, the only workspace packages it may import, and why. */
const LAYERS: Record<string, { dir: string; may: string[]; job: string }> = {
  "@ladder/model": { dir: "packages/model", may: [], job: "model is the shared vocabulary of types; it depends on nothing, ..." },
  "@ladder/gen":   { dir: "packages/gen", may: ["@ladder/model"], job: "gen turns a course folder into a Course; it knows nothing of HTML or the command line" },
  "@ladder/lite":  { dir: "packages/lite", may: ["@ladder/model"], job: "lite turns a Course into HTML; it never reads a course folder, ..." },
  "@ladder/cli":   { dir: "apps/cli", may: ["@ladder/model", "@ladder/gen", "@ladder/lite"], job: "cli is the composition root ..." },
};
```

Each layer carries its **reason**. The reason goes into the failure message, so the person or agent who
meets it learns why, not just what.

---

## How the test reads code

```ts
/** Source without comments, so a comment may name what the code must not do. */
const stripComments = (text: string) => text.replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\/|(?<![:"'])\/\/.*$/gm, "");

const modules: Module[] = Object.entries(LAYERS).flatMap(([layer, { dir }]) =>
  readdirSync(join(ROOT, dir, "src"), { recursive: true, encoding: "utf8" })
    .filter((file) => file.endsWith(".ts"))
    .map((file) => ({ layer, file: `${dir}/src/${file}`, source: stripComments(readFileSync(join(ROOT, dir, "src", file), "utf8")) })),
);

const specifiers = (source: string) => [...source.matchAll(/(?:from|import)\s*\(?\s*"([^"]+)"/g)].map((match) => match[1]!);
```

Plain text and regular expressions, with `node:test`. No parser and no dependency: good enough for a
workspace of four packages whose imports are all `import ... from "..."`.

---

## Six rules

| Rule | Checks | Applies to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Layering | workspace imports are in the layer's `may` list | every file |
| Package boundary | a relative import never leaves its own `src/` | every file |
| Port | no `node:`, `process.`, `console.`, `fetch(`, `require(` or `Deno.` | every layer except `cli` |
| Model | only `export type` and `export interface`; no imports | `model` |
| Closure | `package.json` declares exactly the `@ladder/*` packages the source imports | every package |
| Honest Double | every `implements FileSystemPort` class is in the contract test | every adapter |

The first three run once per file, so a failure names the file. The Honest Double rule came up in talk 2.

---

## A teaching failure: layering

`packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts` gains `import { parseMarkdown } from "@ladder/gen";`

```
not ok 10 - packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts
  Layering rule: @ladder/lite may import only [@ladder/model], because lite turns a Course into HTML;
  it never reads a course folder, so it can render any tutors.json. Move the code to a layer that may
  make this import, or take what you need as an argument; apps/cli wires the layers together
  (README.md, Architecture).
    packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts imports @ladder/gen
not ok 15 - each package.json declares exactly the workspace packages its source imports
  Closure rule: a package's dependencies are exactly what its source imports. Add the missing dependency
  as "workspace:*", or remove the unused one, then run pnpm install.
    packages/lite/package.json declares [@ladder/model] but its src imports [@ladder/gen, @ladder/model]
```

The rule, the offender, the reason, and two ways out. That is L4.

---

## A teaching failure: the port

`packages/gen/src/course-builder.ts` gains `import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";`

```
Port rule: only apps/cli touches node: modules, process, the console or the network. gen receives the
world through ports: gen reads files through FileSystemPort (its `fs` argument) and reports through
BuildOptions.warn; lite returns pages as data for apps/cli to write. The package's tsconfig.json has no
Node types, so tsc rejects this too.
    packages/gen/src/course-builder.ts uses from "node:fs"
```

And, before the tests even run, `pnpm typecheck`:

```
packages/gen/src/course-builder.ts(10,30): error TS2591: Cannot find name 'node:fs'. Do you need to
install type definitions for node? ...
```

Two independent walls: the test (L3/L4) and the compiler (L5, through `types: []`).

---

## The other three, failing

```
Model rule: packages/model holds only `export type` and `export interface`, and imports nothing, so it
adds no code and no dependency to any layer. Put functions and constants in the layer that uses them
(preOrder and folderKinds live in gen).
    packages/model/src/index.ts: export const LO_TYPES = ["course", "topic"];

Package boundary rule: a relative import may not leave packages/lite/src. Import another package by its
name (@ladder/...) through its index.ts, and only if the layering rule allows it.
    packages/lite/src/site-builder.ts imports "../../gen/src/file-system-port.ts"

Honest Double rule: every FileSystemPort adapter runs the same contract, so a test double cannot drift
from the real thing. Add the adapter to ADAPTERS in apps/cli/tests/file-system-port.contract.test.ts.
    ZipFileSystem (packages/gen/src/zip-file-system.ts)
```

Each one was seeded in a scratch copy and caught. You will seed them yourself in the lab.

---

## The Human Boundary around the rules

```markdown
<!-- variants/rung-3-layers/AGENTS.md, "Ask a human first" -->
- Adding or upgrading any dependency, or changing `tests/rung-fence.test.ts`.
- Changing `tests/architecture/`, a package's `tsconfig.json`, or the port's methods.
- Adding a package or app, or changing this file.
```

An agent that meets the Layering rule changes the code. Only a person changes `LAYERS`. The assignment is
exactly such a change: a new package, with you, not the agent, deciding where it sits.

---

## Upstream: dependency-cruiser

```js
// .dependency-cruiser.cjs (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
const LAYERS = [
  { name: "foundation", paths: ["packages/jsr/[^/]+", "packages/svelte/utils/logger", "packages/svelte/utils/metrics"] },
  { name: "core", paths: ["packages/svelte/runes", "packages/svelte/course", ...] },
  ...
  { name: "apps", paths: ["apps/[^/]+"] }
];

const layerRules = LAYERS.slice(0, -1).map((layer, index) => ({
  name: `layer-${layer.name}`,
  comment: `The ${layer.name} layer must not import from a layer above it (...). See the Architecture section of README.md.`,
  from: { path: anyOf(layer.paths) },
  to: { path: anyOf(LAYERS.slice(index + 1).flatMap((l) => l.paths)) }
}));
```

Plus named rules such as `no-app-to-app`, `no-cross-workspace-relative-import` (rung 3's Package boundary
rule) and `course-rules-no-file-system` (rung 3's Port rule). The same ideas, on a real module graph.

---

## Upstream: negative fixtures

```ts
// tests/architecture/dependency-rules.test.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
describe("negative fixtures", () => {
  it.each([
    ["layer-foundation", "packages/jsr/model/src/reaches-up.ts", "packages/svelte/runes/src/index.ts"],
    ["no-app-to-app", "apps/reader/src/lib/borrowed.ts", "apps/catalogue/src/lib/page.ts"],
    ["no-cross-workspace-relative-import", "apps/reader/src/lib/borrowed.ts", "apps/catalogue/src/lib/page.ts"],
    ...
  ])("%s catches %s -> %s", (rule, from, to) => { expect(violations).toContain(`${rule}: ${from} -> ${to}`); });

  it("does not flag downward imports or a cycle inside one package", () => { ... });
});
```

A tiny fake workspace in `tests/architecture/fixtures/workspace` breaks every rule on purpose. The test
proves each rule **can** fail. A rule that never fails might be checking nothing.

---

## Upstream: the ratchet

```
# tests/architecture/known-violations.txt (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
# Architecture violations that predate tier A (rules in .dependency-cruiser.cjs).
# This file may only shrink: the test fails on new violations and on entries
# that no longer occur. Format: `rule: from -> to`.

layer-core: packages/svelte/course/src/course/services/lo-tree.ts -> packages/svelte/themes/src/index.ts
```

```ts
// scripts/checks/lib/ratchet.ts
export function ratchet(current: Iterable<string>, baseline: Iterable<string>): RatchetResult {
  return { added: [...now].filter((e) => !known.has(e)).sort(), stale: [...known].filter((e) => !now.has(e)).sort() };
}
```

New violation: fail ("Fix them rather than adding them to ..."). Fixed violation still listed: fail
("Delete them from ..."). An old codebase adopts a rule without fixing everything first.

---

## Text rules or a graph?

| | Rung 3 (text and regex) | Upstream (dependency-cruiser) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dependency | none | `dependency-cruiser` |
| Sees | `import ... from "..."` in source | the resolved module graph, `.svelte` included |
| Catches type-only imports | yes, they are text | not in `.svelte` files, which the Svelte compiler strips first; a separate text test covers ui-primitives |
| Proves a rule can fail | the lab, by hand | negative fixtures, in the suite |
| Old violations | none allowed | a ratchet baseline |

Rung 3's rule count is small enough for text. Upstream needs the graph. Both put the reason in the message.

---

## Summary

- the README's diagram is L2; the architecture test makes each line L3 with an L4 message
- `types: []` per package makes the Port rule L5 as well
- the reason for each layer lives in the rule, and so in every failure
- upstream: dependency-cruiser rules, negative fixtures that prove each rule bites, and a ratchet

Read the note "Assertion levels in rung 3", then break the layers in the lab.
